-- Q1: easy / L1 — Flat-benefit plan PBO equals ABO
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715, in a flat-benefit defined benefit plan where benefits are a fixed dollar amount per year of service with no salary component, what is the relationship between the PBO and the ABO?', '["The PBO and ABO are equal because there are no future salary increases to project", "The PBO exceeds the ABO by the present value of expected inflation adjustments", "The ABO exceeds the PBO because it includes all vested benefits", "The PBO is lower than the ABO in flat-benefit plans"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-30-35, the PBO differs from the ABO solely because it incorporates projected future salary increases. In a flat-benefit plan where benefits are a fixed dollar amount per year of service, there are no salary increases to project, making the PBO and ABO identical. Choice B is incorrect because flat-benefit formulas do not automatically incorporate inflation adjustments — only salary-related formulas cause the PBO to exceed the ABO.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: easy / L2 — Unfunded DB plan has no expected return component
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Thornberry Corp. establishes a defined benefit pension plan but makes no contributions to fund it. The actuary reports service cost of $200,000 and interest cost of $120,000 for the first year. What is Thornberry''s net periodic pension cost?', '["$200,000 because only service cost is recognized for unfunded plans", "$80,000 because interest cost offsets service cost in unfunded plans", "$320,000 because there is no expected return on plan assets to subtract", "$120,000 because interest cost is the only required component for unfunded plans"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-30-35, net periodic pension cost includes service cost, interest cost, expected return on plan assets, and amortization items. When a plan has no assets, the expected return component is zero, so pension expense equals $200,000 + $120,000 = $320,000. Choice A is incorrect because all applicable components must be recognized regardless of funding status — interest cost accrues on the PBO whether or not the plan holds assets.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Frozen pension plan: zero service cost
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Ridgedale Industries freezes its defined benefit pension plan on January 1, ceasing all benefit accruals. The plan retains a PBO of $6,000,000, plan assets of $5,200,000, and unrecognized net actuarial losses of $520,000. The discount rate is 5% and the expected return on plan assets is 7%. What components of net periodic pension cost does Ridgedale continue to recognize after the freeze?', '["Only amortization of the net actuarial loss, because all other components cease upon plan freeze", "Interest cost on the PBO, expected return on plan assets, and amortization of net actuarial loss", "Service cost, interest cost, and expected return, because the plan still has participants", "Only interest cost, because expected return and amortization cease when benefit accruals stop"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35, freezing a plan eliminates future benefit accruals, reducing service cost to zero. However, the remaining components continue: interest cost accrues on the existing PBO ($6,000,000 x 5% = $300,000), expected return offsets plan asset earnings ($5,200,000 x 7% = $364,000), and corridor amortization of the $520,000 net loss continues if it exceeds the corridor threshold. Choice C is incorrect because service cost requires active benefit accrual — once frozen, no new benefits are earned.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — OPEB implicit rate subsidy from blended premium
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Oakwood Health Group pays a blended healthcare premium of $800 per month for all employees and retirees regardless of age. Actual per-capita claims cost is $600 for active employees (average age 45) and $1,100 for retirees (average age 62). Under ASC 715-60, how should the actuary account for this cost structure when measuring the APBO?', '["Use the $800 blended premium as the benefit cost because that is the employer''s actual expenditure", "Use the $600 active-employee cost for all participants to maintain consistency", "Ignore the cost differential because OPEB measurement uses only the contractual premium rate", "Recognize an implicit rate subsidy by using the $1,100 age-adjusted claims cost for retirees in measuring the APBO"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 715-60-35, when an employer pays the same blended premium for active and retired employees, retirees receive an implicit rate subsidy because their actual claims costs exceed the premium. The actuary must use age-adjusted per-capita claims costs ($1,100 for retirees) rather than the blended premium when measuring the APBO. Choice A is incorrect because the blended premium understates the true cost of providing benefits to retirees and overstates it for active employees.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Compensated absences ASC 710 criteria
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Maplewood Corp. offers employees 10 days of paid sick leave per year. Unused sick days do not accumulate from year to year and do not vest upon termination. At year-end, employees have used only 60% of their sick leave. Under ASC 710-10-25, should Maplewood accrue a liability for the unused sick days?', '["Yes, because the obligation relates to employee services already rendered during the year", "Yes, because the unused sick leave represents a probable future payment", "No, because the sick leave benefit neither vests nor accumulates", "No, because sick leave is excluded from the scope of ASC 710"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 710-10-25, a liability for compensated absences is accrued when four conditions are met: the obligation relates to services already rendered, rights vest or accumulate, payment is probable, and the amount is estimable. Sick leave that neither vests nor accumulates fails the second criterion, so no accrual is required. Choice A is incorrect because meeting only one of the four conditions is insufficient — all four must be satisfied for accrual.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Multiple employer plan vs multiemployer plan
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Sandstone Corp. participates in a pension arrangement with two other unrelated companies. Each employer has a separate account within the plan, and benefits are determined based on each employer''s individual contribution formula. Under ASC 715, how should Sandstone classify and account for this arrangement?', '["As a multiemployer plan, recognizing only annual contributions as pension expense", "As a multiple employer plan, accounting for its individual interest as a single-employer defined benefit plan", "As a jointly governed plan requiring proportionate consolidation of all plan assets and obligations", "As a cost-sharing arrangement, splitting total plan expense equally among the three employers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-80, a multiple employer plan maintains separate accounts for each participating employer with individually determined benefits, unlike a multiemployer plan where assets are pooled. Because Sandstone has a separate account with its own benefit formula, it accounts for its individual interest using single-employer DB plan guidance under ASC 715-30. Choice A is incorrect because the simplified multiemployer treatment applies only when plan assets are commingled and individual employer obligations cannot be separated.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Postemployment benefits ASC 712 scope
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 712, which type of benefits are classified as postemployment benefits?', '["Benefits provided to former employees after employment but before retirement, such as severance pay and disability benefits", "Benefits provided to employees during active employment, such as healthcare and vacation pay", "Benefits provided to retired employees, such as pension payments and retiree healthcare", "Benefits provided to employee dependents during the employee''s active service"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 712-10-05, postemployment benefits are provided to former or inactive employees after employment but before retirement. These include severance pay, disability benefits, job training, and continuation of healthcare or life insurance during the postemployment period. Choice C is incorrect because it describes postretirement benefits, which fall under ASC 715 (pensions) and ASC 715-60 (OPEB), not postemployment benefits under ASC 712.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Retiree-only plan uses life expectancy for amortization
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Stonebridge Corp. closed its defined benefit pension plan to new entrants 20 years ago. All remaining plan participants are now retired with an average remaining life expectancy of 14 years. The plan has unrecognized net actuarial losses exceeding the corridor by $180,000. Under ASC 715-30-35, what period should Stonebridge use to amortize the excess?', '["The original average remaining service period from the plan''s active years", "No amortization is required because the corridor approach ceases when all participants retire", "The average remaining life expectancy of the inactive participants, which is 14 years", "A single year, because immediate recognition is required when no active employees remain"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-21, when all or almost all plan participants are inactive (retired or terminated vested), the amortization period for net actuarial gains and losses is the average remaining life expectancy of inactive participants rather than the average remaining service period. Here, $180,000 / 14 = $12,857 per year. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 715 does not require immediate recognition — it substitutes life expectancy for the service period metric to maintain the delayed recognition approach.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Healthcare cost trend rate grading to ultimate rate
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Prescott Medical Group''s actuary measures the OPEB obligation using a healthcare cost trend rate of 7.5% for the current year, declining by 0.5% annually to an ultimate rate of 5.0%. Under ASC 715-60, what does this grading pattern represent?', '["The contractual increase schedule embedded in the employer''s healthcare insurance premium", "The assumed rate at which per-capita healthcare costs will increase, transitioning from a near-term rate to a long-term equilibrium rate", "The expected inflation rate applied to all postretirement benefit obligations", "The discount rate used to calculate the present value of future OPEB cash flows"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-60-35, the healthcare cost trend rate reflects the expected annual increase in per-capita healthcare claim costs. Actuaries typically assume a higher near-term rate that gradually declines to a lower ultimate rate reflecting long-term medical cost expectations. Here, the rate grades from 7.5% to 5.0% over five years. Choice D is incorrect because the discount rate is a separate assumption — the trend rate projects future benefit amounts, while the discount rate determines their present value.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Multiemployer plan zone status disclosures
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Granville Construction participates in a multiemployer defined benefit pension plan. The plan''s most recent actuarial report indicates the plan is in critical status (red zone) under the Pension Protection Act. The plan''s rehabilitation plan requires a 15% surcharge on all employer contributions. Granville''s base annual contribution is $200,000. Under ASC 715-80, what is the accounting and disclosure effect?', '["Granville must recognize a proportionate share of the plan''s unfunded PBO as a liability", "Granville reports contributions of $200,000 as pension expense and discloses only the base contribution rate", "Granville recognizes $230,000 in pension expense including the surcharge and discloses the plan''s critical zone status", "Granville must exit the multiemployer plan immediately and recognize a withdrawal liability"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-80-50, employers in a multiemployer plan in critical status must disclose the zone classification, required surcharges, and potential impact on future contributions. The 15% surcharge increases Granville''s contribution and expense to $230,000 ($200,000 x 1.15). Choice A is incorrect because multiemployer plan participants generally do not recognize a proportionate share of the plan''s unfunded obligation — they recognize contributions as expense, plus any surcharges imposed by the rehabilitation plan.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Rabbi trust not qualifying as plan assets
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Ashworth Industries establishes a rabbi trust to fund its nonqualified deferred compensation plan for senior executives. The trust holds $3,200,000 in marketable securities. The irrevocable trust agreement specifies that assets are designated to pay deferred compensation but remain subject to the claims of Ashworth''s general creditors in bankruptcy. Under ASC 710-10, how should Ashworth report these amounts?', '["The trust assets are reported as employer assets and the deferred compensation obligation as a liability; the assets do not qualify as plan assets under ASC 715", "The trust assets are classified as plan assets and offset against the deferred compensation liability in a net funded status presentation", "The trust assets are excluded from the balance sheet because they are held by an independent trustee", "The trust assets are recognized as a prepaid benefit cost reducing the deferred compensation expense over the vesting period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 710-10-25, rabbi trust assets remain subject to the employer''s general creditors in bankruptcy, so they do not meet the plan asset definition under ASC 715-30-35 (which requires assets to be segregated and restricted solely for benefit payments beyond creditor reach). The employer reports trust investments as its own assets and separately reports the full deferred compensation liability. Choice B is incorrect because funded status netting requires assets legally beyond the employer''s creditors'' reach.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Plan asset fair value hierarchy disclosure
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Warwick Corp.''s defined benefit plan holds $4,000,000 in publicly traded equities with quoted prices, $2,500,000 in corporate bonds valued using broker-quoted yield curves, and $1,500,000 in a private real estate fund valued using a discounted cash flow model with unobservable inputs. Under ASC 715-20-50, how must Warwick disclose the fair value of plan assets?', '["At a single aggregate fair value with a description of the overall investment policy", "By broad asset category without fair value hierarchy classification", "At aggregate fair value with a sensitivity analysis showing the effect of key assumption changes", "By fair value hierarchy level: $4,000,000 as Level 1, $2,500,000 as Level 2, and $1,500,000 as Level 3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 715-20-50-1(d), employers must disclose plan assets by major category and classify them within the ASC 820 fair value hierarchy. Publicly traded equities with quoted market prices are Level 1, corporate bonds priced using observable yield curves are Level 2, and the private real estate fund using unobservable inputs is Level 3. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 715-20-50 specifically requires fair value hierarchy classification — disclosure by asset category alone is insufficient.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — OPEB curtailment with PSC acceleration
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Lakeside Health System closes its outpatient division, eliminating future service for 120 employees. The OPEB plan has an APBO of $8,500,000, unamortized prior service cost of $380,000 in AOCI from a benefit enhancement three years ago, and plan assets of $2,100,000. The curtailment reduces the APBO by $1,200,000. Under ASC 715-60-35, what is the net curtailment effect?', '["A curtailment gain of $1,200,000 with continued PSC amortization over the remaining service period", "A net curtailment gain of $820,000 after offsetting the $380,000 PSC acceleration against the $1,200,000 APBO reduction", "A curtailment loss of $380,000 for the accelerated PSC with the APBO reduction deferred in OCI", "A curtailment gain of $1,580,000 combining the APBO reduction with elimination of the prior service cost"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-60-35, a curtailment requires two calculations: (1) accelerated recognition of unamortized PSC related to eliminated future service, creating a $380,000 loss reclassified from AOCI to expense, and (2) the $1,200,000 APBO reduction creating a gain. The net curtailment gain is $1,200,000 minus $380,000 = $820,000. Choice D is incorrect because the PSC acceleration is a loss that offsets the APBO reduction gain — it is not added to create a larger gain.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — OPEB plans typically unfunded
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715-60, what distinguishes the funding status of most OPEB plans from defined benefit pension plans?', '["OPEB plans are required to be fully funded under ERISA, similar to pension plans", "OPEB plans must maintain plan assets equal to at least 80% of the APBO", "OPEB plans are often unfunded or only partially funded because ERISA generally does not mandate OPEB funding", "OPEB plans are always fully funded because employers pre-fund retiree healthcare costs through insurance premiums"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-60, a key distinction is funding. ERISA mandates minimum funding for defined benefit pension plans but generally does not require employers to pre-fund OPEB obligations such as retiree healthcare. As a result, most OPEB plans are unfunded or underfunded, producing large liabilities on the balance sheet with no offsetting plan assets. Choice A is incorrect because ERISA minimum funding requirements apply primarily to qualified pension plans, not OPEB plans.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — PSC from plan amendment initially recognized in OCI
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Fenwick Industries amends its defined benefit plan on July 1 to retroactively increase the benefit formula. The actuary determines the amendment creates $480,000 in prior service cost. Fenwick''s fiscal year ends December 31. At the time of the amendment, how should Fenwick initially recognize the prior service cost?', '["Record $480,000 as a charge to other comprehensive income and a credit to the pension liability, with no immediate effect on pension expense", "Record $480,000 as pension expense in the period of the amendment", "Record $240,000 as pension expense for the half-year and defer the remaining $240,000", "Record $480,000 as a deferred asset to be amortized against future pension expense"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-30-25-11, prior service cost from a plan amendment is recognized immediately in other comprehensive income (debit to OCI loss) with a corresponding increase in the pension liability. The PSC is then amortized from AOCI to pension expense over the average remaining service period of employees expected to benefit. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 715 uses delayed recognition for prior service cost — immediate expensing is not permitted.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Healthcare cost trend rate is OPEB-specific
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715, which actuarial assumption is required for measuring OPEB obligations but is NOT used in defined benefit pension plan accounting?', '["Discount rate based on high-quality corporate bond yields", "Expected long-term rate of return on plan assets", "Mortality rates and expected employee turnover", "Healthcare cost trend rate reflecting expected increases in per-capita medical costs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 715-60-35, the healthcare cost trend rate — the expected annual rate of increase in per-capita healthcare claim costs — is a critical assumption for measuring OPEB obligations. This assumption has no counterpart in pension accounting because pension benefits are salary-based, not healthcare-based. Choice A is incorrect because the discount rate is required for both pension and OPEB obligation measurements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Curtailment definition
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715, what constitutes a curtailment of a defined benefit pension plan?', '["An irrevocable transfer of pension obligations to an insurance company through annuity purchases", "An event that significantly reduces expected future service or eliminates the accrual of defined benefits for a significant number of employees", "A plan amendment that retroactively increases benefits for employees with prior service", "A change in actuarial assumptions that increases the projected benefit obligation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-14, a curtailment occurs when an event significantly reduces expected future years of service of active participants or eliminates the accrual of defined benefits for a significant portion of future service, such as a plant closing or workforce reduction. Choice A is incorrect because it describes a settlement — the irrevocable discharge of the pension obligation — which is a distinct event from a curtailment, though both may occur simultaneously.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: hard / L3 — Cannot net pension asset with OPEB liability
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Westbridge Corp. sponsors an overfunded defined benefit pension plan with plan assets exceeding the PBO by $1,800,000 and a wholly unfunded OPEB plan with an APBO of $2,600,000. The CFO proposes reporting a single net postretirement liability of $800,000 on the balance sheet. Under ASC 715, is this netting approach acceptable?', '["No, the pension asset and OPEB liability must be presented separately because they are obligations of different benefit plans", "Yes, netting is required when both plans cover the same employee group", "Yes, netting is permitted at the employer''s discretion as long as it is disclosed in the notes", "No, but only because the two plans use different actuarial assumptions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-30-45 and ASC 715-60-45, the funded status of each benefit plan must be recognized separately on the balance sheet. A pension plan asset cannot be offset against an OPEB liability because they represent distinct benefit arrangements with separate obligations. Choice D is incorrect because the prohibition on netting is based on the separate-plan principle, not differences in actuarial assumptions — even plans with identical assumptions cannot be netted.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Plan asset allocation affects expected return assumption
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Centennial Corp.''s defined benefit plan holds 70% equities (9% expected return), 20% bonds (4% expected return), and 10% real estate (7% expected return). The board proposes shifting to 30% equities, 60% bonds, and 10% real estate. Under ASC 715-30-35, what is the most likely effect on the expected return used in net periodic pension cost?', '["No effect, because the expected return assumption is set at plan inception and does not change", "The expected return increases because fixed-income securities provide more stable returns", "The expected return decreases because the shift toward lower-returning fixed-income reduces the weighted-average rate", "The expected return is eliminated because a conservative allocation signals declining investment expectations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-47, the expected long-term rate of return should reflect anticipated returns based on the plan''s current and target asset allocation. Shifting from 70% equities to 30% equities with 60% bonds reduces the weighted-average expected return from approximately 7.8% to 5.8%, lowering the expected return component that offsets pension expense and increasing net periodic pension cost. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 715 requires the expected return to be updated when material changes in investment policy occur.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Graded vesting definition
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under a graded vesting schedule for a defined benefit pension plan, how do employees become entitled to their accrued pension benefits?', '["All benefits vest immediately upon plan enrollment", "All benefits vest at once after a specified number of years of service", "Benefits vest only upon reaching the plan''s normal retirement age", "Benefits vest incrementally over a period of service, with an increasing percentage vesting each year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 715-30 and ERISA Section 203, a graded vesting schedule allows employees to earn rights to accrued pension benefits incrementally — for example, 20% after two years, 40% after three years, until fully vested. This differs from cliff vesting where 100% vests at once after a specified period. Choice B is incorrect because it describes cliff vesting, not graded vesting — cliff vesting provides no partial entitlement before the threshold date.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Net periodic pension cost can be negative
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715, is it possible for an employer to report negative net periodic pension cost (a net pension credit) for a defined benefit plan?', '["No, net periodic pension cost must always be a positive amount representing the employer''s obligation", "Yes, when the expected return on plan assets and gain amortizations exceed the combined service cost, interest cost, and loss amortizations", "No, because service cost always exceeds the expected return on plan assets", "Yes, but only in the year of plan termination when settlement gains are recognized"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35, the five components of net periodic pension cost can produce a net credit when the expected return on plan assets and any gain amortization exceed service cost, interest cost, prior service cost amortization, and loss amortization combined. This commonly occurs with well-funded plans having large asset bases. Choice A is incorrect because the expected return component can dominate when plan assets substantially exceed the PBO.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Required PBO reconciliation disclosure
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Whitmore Corp.''s external auditor requests detailed pension disclosures for the year-end financial statements. Under ASC 715-20-50, which reconciliation schedule is required in the notes for a defined benefit pension plan?', '["A reconciliation of beginning and ending PBO balances showing service cost, interest cost, actuarial gains or losses, benefits paid, and plan amendments", "A reconciliation of net periodic pension cost to the employer''s cash contributions for the year", "A reconciliation of ERISA minimum funding requirements to GAAP pension expense", "A reconciliation of the corridor calculation showing the determination of required amortization"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-20-50-1(b), employers must disclose a reconciliation of beginning and ending PBO balances, identifying separately service cost, interest cost, participant contributions, actuarial gains and losses, benefits paid, plan amendments, and business combinations. Choice C is incorrect because ERISA funding calculations are regulatory requirements separate from GAAP — ASC 715 does not require reconciliation between the two frameworks.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Pension expense as noncash charge on cash flow statement
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Hargrove Corp. reports net periodic pension cost of $420,000 under ASC 715 but makes no cash contribution to the pension plan trust during the year. On the statement of cash flows prepared under the indirect method, how is pension expense treated?', '["As an operating cash outflow of $420,000", "No cash flow adjustment is needed because pension expense is already in net income", "As a noncash operating charge added back to net income because no cash was paid", "As a financing activity because pension obligations are long-term liabilities"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 230, when pension expense exceeds cash contributions (here, contributions are zero), the noncash portion of pension expense is effectively added back to net income under the indirect method because it reduced net income without a corresponding cash outflow. The increase in pension liability represents a noncash operating adjustment. Choice A is incorrect because no cash was disbursed — the $420,000 expense is entirely an accrual requiring an add-back.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — OPEB negative PSC from benefit reduction amendment
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Ridgefield Health Group amends its OPEB plan to eliminate prescription drug coverage, reducing the APBO by $1,400,000. The plan has existing unamortized prior service cost of $560,000 in AOCI from a benefit enhancement adopted four years ago. The average remaining service period of affected employees is 9 years. Under ASC 715-60-25, how should Ridgefield account for this amendment?', '["Recognize a $1,400,000 gain immediately in pension expense", "Record negative PSC of $1,400,000 in OCI and amortize as a credit over 9 years, with the existing $560,000 continuing its original schedule", "Offset the $1,400,000 against the existing $560,000 and recognize the $840,000 excess immediately as a gain", "Record negative PSC of $1,400,000 in OCI, first offsetting the existing $560,000 PSC balance, then amortizing the remaining $840,000 credit over 9 years"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 715-60-25, a plan amendment reducing benefits creates negative prior service cost recognized in OCI. The negative PSC of $1,400,000 first offsets existing unamortized PSC in AOCI ($560,000), and the remaining $840,000 credit is amortized over the 9-year average remaining service period as a reduction of OPEB expense. Choice C is incorrect because the excess is not recognized immediately — ASC 715 requires amortization of the net PSC balance over the remaining service period.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — MRV smoothing dampens expected return volatility
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Waverly Manufacturing uses a five-year market-related value (MRV) of plan assets to compute the expected return component of pension cost. The fair value of plan assets dropped 15% during a market downturn and recovered 20% the following year. Under ASC 715-30-35, what is the primary effect of using MRV instead of fair value for the expected return calculation?', '["The expected return component is less volatile year-to-year because asset gains and losses are smoothed over five years", "The expected return is always higher than using fair value because MRV eliminates investment losses", "The expected return is always lower because MRV lags behind current fair value during recoveries", "The MRV and fair value produce identical expected returns over any five-year period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-47, employers may use a market-related value that recognizes asset gains and losses over a period up to five years, smoothing short-term market volatility. During the downturn, MRV does not fully reflect the 15% decline; during recovery, it does not immediately capture the 20% gain, producing more stable expected return amounts. Choice B is incorrect because MRV does not eliminate losses — it merely delays their full recognition, and MRV may lag fair value during recoveries.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Curtailment recognized before settlement
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Meridian Manufacturing closes a major plant, resulting in both a curtailment (future service eliminated for 200 employees) and a settlement (lump-sum distributions of $4,500,000 to terminated participants). The plan has a PBO of $18,000,000, plan assets of $15,000,000, unamortized PSC of $600,000, and net actuarial loss of $1,800,000 in AOCI. Under ASC 715-30-35, in what sequence must these events be recognized?', '["The settlement is recognized first, followed by the curtailment, because cash distributions take precedence", "The curtailment is recognized first including PSC acceleration and PBO reduction, then the settlement gain or loss is calculated using post-curtailment balances", "Both events are combined and recognized as a single net gain or loss in the period", "The curtailment and settlement are recognized simultaneously with no required ordering"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-18, when a curtailment and settlement occur together, the curtailment is recognized first: unamortized PSC attributable to eliminated future service is accelerated, and the PBO reduction is recognized. The settlement gain or loss is then calculated using post-curtailment PBO and AOCI balances. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 715 explicitly requires curtailment accounting to precede settlement accounting to ensure the settlement references adjusted amounts.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Current vs noncurrent pension liability classification
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Kendall Corp.''s defined benefit plan has a PBO of $9,000,000 and plan assets of $7,200,000 at year-end. The actuary estimates $310,000 in pension benefits will be paid within the next 12 months. The plan''s assets are sufficient to cover these near-term payments. Under ASC 715-30-45, how should Kendall classify the $1,800,000 net pension liability?', '["$310,000 as current and $1,490,000 as noncurrent based on near-term benefits payable", "Entirely as a current liability because the plan is underfunded", "Entirely as a noncurrent liability because plan assets are sufficient to cover benefits due within 12 months", "50% current and 50% noncurrent based on the ratio of plan assets to PBO"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-30-45, the current portion of an underfunded pension liability equals the amount by which benefits expected to be paid in the next 12 months exceed plan assets. Because Kendall''s plan assets ($7,200,000) far exceed the $310,000 in near-term benefits, no current liability exists — the entire $1,800,000 is classified as noncurrent. Choice A is incorrect because current classification requires near-term benefit payments to exceed plan assets, which is not the case here.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — SFAS 158 eliminated additional minimum liability
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under current GAAP as codified in ASC 715 after the adoption of SFAS 158, what is reported on the balance sheet for a defined benefit pension plan?', '["The accumulated benefit obligation net of plan assets and any additional minimum liability adjustment", "The projected benefit obligation reduced by an intangible asset for unrecognized prior service cost", "The greater of the net pension liability or the additional minimum liability", "The full funded status — the difference between plan assets at fair value and the PBO"]'::jsonb, 3, 'SFAS 158 (codified in ASC 715-30-25) eliminated the previous additional minimum liability framework and requires employers to recognize the full funded status (fair value of plan assets minus PBO) as either a net asset or net liability on the balance sheet. All previously unrecognized items are reported in accumulated other comprehensive income. Choice A is incorrect because the additional minimum liability concept was eliminated — current GAAP uses only the funded status approach.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Discount rate change: competing effects on interest cost
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Fairmont Corp.''s actuary reduces the discount rate from 5.5% to 4.5% at year-end. Before the change, the PBO is $20,000,000 and plan assets are $18,000,000. The rate reduction increases the PBO by $2,200,000 to $22,200,000. Under ASC 715, what is the comprehensive effect on the financial statements?', '["The net pension liability increases by $2,200,000, an actuarial loss of $2,200,000 is recognized in OCI, and next year''s interest cost decreases because the lower rate more than offsets the higher PBO base", "The net pension liability increases by $2,200,000, pension expense increases by $2,200,000 immediately in earnings", "The net pension liability is unchanged because discount rate changes are applied prospectively only", "The net pension liability increases by $2,200,000, an actuarial loss of $2,200,000 is recognized in OCI, and next year''s interest cost increases proportionally with the higher PBO"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-30-35, the discount rate reduction increases the PBO by $2,200,000, creating an actuarial loss recognized in OCI. The net pension liability rises from $2,000,000 to $4,200,000. Counterintuitively, next year''s interest cost decreases: the lower rate applied to the higher PBO (4.5% x $22,200,000 = $999,000) produces less than the prior calculation (5.5% x $20,000,000 = $1,100,000). Choice D is incorrect because the lower discount rate more than offsets the higher PBO base in this scenario.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Turnover assumption change effect on PBO
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Bellingham Corp.''s actuary revises the expected employee turnover rate downward from 8% to 5% per year, reflecting improved retention. Under ASC 715, what is the most likely effect of this assumption change on the pension plan?', '["The PBO decreases because fewer employees will leave before vesting", "The PBO increases because more employees are expected to remain and earn benefits, creating an actuarial loss in OCI", "Net periodic pension cost increases immediately because the assumption change is treated as a plan amendment", "The funded status is unaffected because turnover assumptions do not influence the PBO"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35, the PBO reflects the present value of expected future benefit payments incorporating demographic assumptions including employee turnover. Lower expected turnover means more employees will stay long enough to earn and receive benefits, increasing the PBO and creating an actuarial loss recognized in other comprehensive income. Choice C is incorrect because assumption changes generate actuarial gains or losses in OCI, not plan amendments — they do not immediately affect pension expense.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
